At Wed, 5 Oct 2005 16:34:47 -0700 (PDT),
Tom Watson wrote:
> 
> --- Takashi Iwai <tiwai@suse.de> wrote:
> 
> > At Tue, 4 Oct 2005 15:18:36 -0700 (PDT),
> > Tom Watson wrote:
> > > 
> > > In my musings, I have found that the M-Audio Revolution 7.1 card seems to
> > only
> > > support the following rates:
> > > 32000, 44100, 48000, 64000, 88200, 96000, 176400, and 19200.
> > > These rates are from a small program I wrote using the
> > > "snd_pcm_hw_params_test_rate" routine, iterating by one from 1 to 192000.
> > > This is in contrast to the mixer control "Multi Track Internal Clock" (as
> > > shown on 'alsamixer' that indicates the following additional rates:
> > > 8000, 9600, 11025, 12000, 16000, 22050, and 24000.
> > > 
> > > What could be the reason for this mismatch?  Shouldn't they be the same?
> > > Is this a bug?
> > 
> > Internally ice1724 chip supports all the rates above while the codec
> > chip on revo 7.1 supports 32kHz or more.  The "Multi Track" control is
> > provided for the internal chip control.
> > 
> > It doesn't make sense to the unsupported value, though, except for
> > debugging/experimental use.
> > 
> > 
> > Takashi
> 
> It seems that the codecs support the rates as well.  I did some experimenting
> and added the rates to the code in the 'revo.c' part of the driver:
> 
> static unsigned int rates[] = {
>         /* Line added below */
>         8000, 9600, 11025, 12000, 16000, 22050, 24000,
>         32000, 44100, 48000, 64000, 88200, 96000,
>         176400, 192000,
> };
> 
> This seemed to accomidate my sound file that was sampled (8 bits, but not
> revelant here) at 11025.  I could very easily use aplay:
> $ aplay -D plug:front soundfile.wav
> And all worked just fine.  Then I tired to make it come out the rear channels
> by using:
> $ aplay -D plug:rear soundfile.wav
> And something "really wierd" happened.  The first 1/2 second or so was PERFECT,
> then it went into some noise.  While I understand that this is obviously not
> supported (even the Revolution documentation on their web site says rates start
> at 32000), could this be something more serious?

You seem right.  I took a look at AK4381/4355 datasheet again, and
found out that it supports 8-192kHz.
Then let's get rid of the restriction.


Thanks,

Takashi


